speechtext:
I am sure my hon. friend will be glad to hear it all because it is signed by J. J. Kinley, the president of the Liberal association of Nova Scotia. It is as follows:
Whereas, during the last number of years, there has been imported into Canada a huge
o.uantity of coal, occasioning the export of many millions of dollars therefor;
_ And whereas .thousands of tons of foreign iron and steel are being annually imported into this country in direct competition with Canadian industry, while the output of our Canadian plants is less than half their capacity, occasioning the export of many additional millions of dollars;
And whereas the Duncan commission on maritime claims, recognizing the importance of Nova Scotia's steel and coal industry as a national asset, recommended that certain measures be introduced immediately with a view to the establishment of these industries;
Therefore be it resolved that a general policy in connection with the steel and coal industries should be adopted by the parliament of Canada, the aim of such policy being the production in Canada of such ooal and steel consumed in Canada, as far as that is possible at the present time; and to take the necessary steps to ensure the expanding of these industries so that eventually Canada may, through them, be enabled to supply its entire requirements;
Further resolved that the recommendations of the Duncan commission made on behalf of these industries be implemented immediately;
Then follows a paragraph containing a list of gentlemen to whom .copies of the resolution were to be sent. This resolution comes from the Lunenburg Board of Trade and is signed by J. J. Kinley, president. He is a Liberal member of the local legislature and I think has occupied the position of acting leader of the opposition in the provincial house during the absence of the leader, Mr. Ohiisholm. It will be very clear to the house that Liberals and Conservatives, boards of trade and the people generally throughout the province of Nova Scotia and I believe throughout the maritime provinces as well, expect these recommendations together with every other recommendation contained in the Duncan report to be implemented without further delay.
There is one other matter I would like to bring up. The other evening I listened to one of the members from Prince Edward Island who spoke in glowing terms of the condition of agriculture in that province. Not long ago I noticed in one of the papers coming from the island that Prince Edward Island farmers were getting very much disturbed and perturbed .over the influx into that province of so many California carrots and so much New Zealand butter, so I do not think the island farmer is of the same opinion as the hon. .member who attempted to speak
on his behalf. Let me say that no matter what the conditions are in Prince Edward Island or the rest of Canada the condition of agriculture and particularly of the dairy industry in Nova Scotia has been made almost intolerable by the influx of New Zealand butter. The herds have been depleted, the swine industry has been affected, the poultry industry has suffered, and generally speaking the whole fabric of agriculture in Nova Scotia has been injured by the influx of this New Zealand butter. The dairymens' associations of the province of Nova Scotia have been passing resolutions for the past three years which have been forwarded to every member, both Liberal and Conservative, from that province. These resolutions have asked the Dominion government to cancel the order in council which permitted New Zealand butter to come into this country under the Australian treaty.
